 Prefix Sums · USACO Guide · Silver
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  ## Introduction

  Given an array a[1], a[2], …, a[N], a prefix sum array stores pₖ = ∑ᵢ₌₁ᵏ aᵢ for every k, with p₀
  = 0. Any range sum is then a difference of two prefix sums:

  aₗ + aₗ₊₁ + ⋯ + aᵣ = pᵣ - pₗ₋₁

  Building p takes O(N) and each query O(1), so Q queries cost O(N + Q) instead of O(NQ).

  ## Implementation

    #include <bits/stdc++.h>
    using namespace std;

    int main() {
        int n, q;
        cin >> n >> q;
        vector<long long> p(n + 1);
        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            long long a;
            cin >> a;
            p[i] = p[i - 1] + a;
        }
        while (q--) {
            int l, r;
            cin >> l >> r;
            cout << p[r] - p[l - 1] << "\n";  // sum of a[l..r]
        }
    }

  ## Pitfalls

  • Sums overflow int quickly: N = 2 × 10⁵ values up to 10⁹
  reach 2 × 10¹⁴. Use long long.
  • Off by one: with 1-indexed p, the range [l, r] is pᵣ - pₗ₋₁.

